<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Philip II of Macedon</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. 359–<a href="/years/336-bce">336 BCE</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/4th-century-bce">4th century BCE</a> · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argead-dynasty">Argead dynasty</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Philip II was king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-macedon">Macedon</a> from 359 to 336 BCE and the father of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a>. He professionalised the Macedonian army, introduced the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sarissa">sarissa</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phalanx">phalanx</a> and used diplomacy and conquest to dominate the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greek-city-states">Greek city-states</a>. His reforms laid the groundwork for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/macedonian-empire">Macedonian Empire</a>, and he was assassinated in 336 BCE.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/philip-ii-of-macedon.jpg" alt="Philip II of Macedon" width="318" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Richard Mortel from Riyadh, Saudi Arabia · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Phillip_II,_king_of_Macedonia,_Roman_copy_of_Greek_original,_Ny_Carlsberg_Glyptotek,_Copenhagen_(36420294055).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Athens, urged on by Demosthenes, joined Thebes against <b>Philip II of Macedon</b> and was defeated at the Battle of Chaeronea in 338 BCE; Philip treated the city leniently, and it joined his League of Corinth.</blockquote><a href="/empire/athens">Athens · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>The young <b>Philip II of Macedon</b> spent several years as a hostage at Thebes in the 360s BCE, at the height of Theban power, and probably learned from its generals.</blockquote><a href="/empire/boeotian-league">Boeotian League · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>The city's most celebrated feat was its resistance to <b>Philip II of Macedon</b>, who besieged it in 340–339 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/empire/byzantium">Byzantium · Military</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Philip II of Macedon</b> defeated Onomarchus at the Battle of the Crocus Field in 352 BCE and imposed a settlement in 346 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/empire/delphi">Delphi · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Cotys I fought Athens for the Thracian Chersonese in the 360s BCE, but divided Thracian forces could not withstand the reformed army of <b>Philip II of Macedon</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/odrysian-kingdom">Odrysian Kingdom · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Smiths forged long iron swords and the iron tyres of chariots, and from the 3rd century BCE many peoples struck coins, first copying the gold staters of <b>Philip II of Macedon</b> and then turning the king’s head and chariot into ever more abstract designs.</blockquote><a href="/culture/celts">Celts · Arts &amp; Knowledge</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in Aigai, in what is now <a href="/countries/greece">Greece</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=40.48,22.32&amp;year=-348" aria-label="Aigai on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5098 717 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Philip II of Macedon"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5548 987h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5548 987h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=40.48,22.32&amp;year=-348">What was here in 348 BCE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;O my son, look thee out a kingdom equal to and worthy of thyself, for Macedonia is too little for thee.&#8221;</p><footer>Plutarch, Life of Alexander &#183; tr. John Dryden and others, rev. A. H. Clough, 1859 &#183; to his son after the boy tamed the horse Bucephalus; Plutarch's account &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/674/pg674-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 535 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
